  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Wed Jun 24 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper trough with several embedded vorts continues to rotate\nover northern MN, bringing scattered showers and thunderstorms\nto northwestern and central MN. Convective activity will taper\noff this evening as the upper trough pulls east and we get\nnorthwesterly flow setting in. Flow becomes near zonal as we\nmove into tomorrow and tomorrow night, with southeasterly winds\nat the surface returning ahead of the next system. The main\nlongwave trough will be digging into the western CONUS Friday\nand into the weekend, and while there are slight differences in\nexactly where the ridge axis to our east and trough axis to the\nwest sets up, there is good agreement on southwesterly flow\naloft setting up. That southwesterly flow aloft will continue\ninto the upcoming work week, bringing warmer temperatures and a\nmore active pattern as several weak shortwaves come through. \n\n...Funnels and small hail potential this afternoon and early \nevening...\n\nWith the upper low still rotating just to our east and plenty of\nheating this afternoon, showers and thunderstorms have\ndeveloped, with highest coverage across northwestern MN. A fair\namount of low level vorticity is also around under the surface\ntrough, and the non-supercell tornado parameter is 2 to 4 in\nportions of the far northern Red River Valley into northwestern\nMN. There has already been one funnel cloud reported near\nGoodridge, MN, and many of the cells in the cluster over\nnorthwestern MN are showing some very weak rotation. Any of the\nupdrafts that develop could produce some funnels or even a brief\ntouchdown, but are expected to remain very weak. Have the\nspecial weather statement to cover these type of situations.\nWith -20 C heights fairly low, some small hail is not out of the\nquestion with a few of the stronger cells. Funnel and small hail\nthreat will rapidly diminish after early evening as we lose \nheating and the upper trough moves further east. \n\n...Severe potential this weekend and next week...\n\nWith upper flow transitioning to southwesterly, the pattern\nlooks to be warmer and more active with several weak shortwaves\ncoming through. The timing of these shortwaves is still not\nresolvable at this point, but several rounds of thunderstorms\nlook possible. The position of the ridge axis would make it seem\nthat the best chances for Saturday afternoon and tonight for\nsevere will be over the western Dakotas, but can't rule out some\nconvection impacting the Devils Lake Basin that night. CWASP\n50th percentile probabilities of 60 or more transition a bit\nfurther east for Sunday into next week. However, much will\ndepend on timing of weak lead shortwaves and how much\ndestabilization we get. Pattern looks active but details\nuncertain at this point.",
